    ]},

  { num:"3", color:"#34D399", title:"Workflow",
    subtitle:"How one edit turns into every Hook + CTA version you deliver.",
    lesson:
`**What this module does:** shows how one edit becomes every Hook + CTA version you deliver.

## Two kinds of voice you'll make
- A **talking-head avatar** that you generate on AI tools in parts.
- A **voiceover** that you'll add b-rolls onto later.

## Step 1
Generate every talking clip / the whole voiceover in one go: all your Hooks, the Body (the main part), and all your CTAs. Not sure how to say a word? Check it on Google.

## Step 2 — line them up
Drop them all onto one timeline in order — all Hooks, then the Body, then all CTAs. This is your **master timeline**.
[[diagram:timeline]]

> Now edit the whole batch together, using what you've learned / will learn in the other modules.

## Step 3 — nest the Body
Bundle the finished Body into a single clip (this is called **nesting**). Include the A-roll, b-roll, voiceover, sound effects, and captions — but **not** the background music. Name it clearly, e.g. \`Body_Batch_Brand\`.

## Step 4 — copy for each combination
Duplicate the **master sequence** for every Hook + CTA pair. Name each copy like \`HOOK 1 Batch 23 CTA 3\`.
[[diagram:nesting]]

## Step 5 — trim & export
In each copy, keep one Hook + the Body + one CTA, delete the rest, then **export at 30fps, 1080p**.

## Why nesting the Body is worth it
- Any change to the Body updates **every version automatically**.
- New elements you add (text, overlays) apply **everywhere**.
- Saves time and stops fixes from being missed across edits.`,
