  { num:"5", color:"#38BDF8", title:"AI Avatars",
    subtitle:"Choosing a face, keeping it real, and going hyper-real with Google OMNI.",
    lesson:
`An avatar that looks fake ends the ad instantly — and this isn't only for health/supplement brands, it applies across all our brands. Always **follow the reference** for how the avatar should look. Avatars must look natural (skin texture, realistic lighting, micro-expressions), never plastic or glassy.

## Two platforms
- **Heygen** — the primary tool, for longer videos where realism isn't critical.
- **Google OMNI** — the most realistic, but ~10 seconds per generation.

## Finding a frame
Prefer **UGC-style** people. The frame needs **eyes open and visible, lips visible and natural, a well-lit and sharp face, no motion blur, and hands visible**. The screenshot must have **no subtitles or graphics** — clean any text with **Nano Banana Pro** (best used in **Google Flow**, though that's not the only place).

AI-generated avatars should avoid glassy skin and texture-less faces — choose natural imperfection: **natural lines, redness, spots, freckles**, etc.

## Google OMNI
Google's latest, best in Flow, **10-second limit**. It works like VEO but **doesn't take a last frame**, so OMNI clips can't be chained. That last-frame chaining works for **VEO 3.1 and Kling**.

> OMNI's limits can be worked around, but the workarounds are time-consuming for now.

## Making the clips
Whichever you use, your prompt should include **no cuts, no transitions, and no added subtitles** — this applies to **both Frames-to-Video and Ingredients-to-Video**.
- **Frames-to-Video is preferable.**
- **Ingredients-to-Video** blends three images, but the **first frame and the overall background might change**.`,
