    ]},

  { num:"6", color:"#FBBF24", title:"B-Rolls & Realistic AI Images",
    subtitle:"Where to source visuals, which model to reach for, and how to use them well.",
    lesson:
`B-rolls turn abstract claims into things people can see — they make a mechanism instantly understandable.

## Search order
- Check the **internal B-Roll Folder** first (if you have been given one).
- Then **TikTok** for real, authentic footage — strip captions and watermarks with **VMAKE**.

## Where the tools live
**Google Flow** is a Google platform that offers Google's own models cheaply. It's the **preferable** place for the Google models (VEO, OMNI, Nano Banana Pro) — but it's **not the only place**.
- **Google OMNI** — Google's latest, 10-second limit; works like VEO but doesn't take a last frame (can't be chained).
- **Nano Banana Pro** — the go-to for editing existing images: swaps, product placement, removing text/logos.
- **Kling 3** — best for talking-head UGC and animations, with more directional control; struggles beyond ~6s for talking heads due to lip-sync. **Kling 3 does not live on Google Flow** — use **Higgsfield** for Kling (and other non-Google models when needed).

## Using b-rolls well
- Every b-roll must **directly support the narration** — every second should add value.
- Match the b-roll to the **target demographic** (age, gender, race). If in doubt, **ask the strategist**.
- Keep each b-roll on screen for about **2.5–3 seconds**.
- Ideally **one b-roll covers one complete phrase or sentence** — don't change visuals mid-phrase unless it improves clarity.
- Frame so the main subject sits roughly at **eye level** — the viewer's eye tends to land on the **upper half of the screen**.
- If a shot feels **static or flat**, apply a **linear continuous zoom** across its entire duration.

> A dedicated SOP for animations is coming later.

## Generating real-looking footage
Start from a reference — a reference **image from Pinterest** or a reference **video from TikTok** — and replace the subject with your avatar and/or surroundings. If you generate directly in an AI tool with no reference, it comes out **polished, yellowish, and flat**, so always feed it a realistic reference.

> Real photos have imperfections — texture, uneven light, small blemishes. Over-polished looks fake.`,
