  { num:"7", color:"#A3E635", title:"Transitions & Zooms",
    subtitle:"When to use them, when not to, and the short list that covers most ads.",
    lesson:
`Transitions and zooms are **not mandatory** — a good ad can work without them.

Whether you use them depends on the brief: if it says **replicate the inspiration exactly** and that ad has none, don't add them. When you have creative freedom, they add rhythm and hold attention.

## The short list
- **Slide left, zoom in/out** — smooth directional flow.
- **Light leak** — a natural visual break that softens a cut.

## Zooms
Mostly **Linear Zoom-Ins** to emphasize a key point, product, or emotional moment — a silent signal that says *"pay attention here."* Zoom-outs are rarely needed, only for a reveal or wider perspective. Getting the **flow and timing** right matters more than the effect itself.`,
    questions:[
      {q:"Are transitions and zooms required in every ad?",options:["No — only when the brief gives creative freedom","Yes, always","Only in static ads","Only for older audiences"],answer:0,explain:"They're optional; use them with creative freedom, not when told to copy exactly."},
      {q:"The brief says replicate the inspiration exactly, and it has no transitions. You should…",options:["Not add any transitions or zooms","Add whip transitions for energy","Add a few zooms anyway","Add light leaks between every cut"],answer:0,explain:"Replicate exactly means don't introduce effects the reference doesn't have."},
      {q:"The most-used zoom, and its purpose, is…",options:["Linear Zoom-In, to emphasize a key point ('pay attention here')","Zoom-out, to reveal a wider scene","Random zoom, to add chaos","Snap zoom, to end the ad"],answer:0,explain:"Linear Zoom-Ins quietly direct attention to important moments."},
      {q:"What is a Light leak transition used for?",options:["To soften a cut / create a natural visual break","To speed up the footage","To add captions","To emphasize a key point"],answer:0,explain:"Light leak transitions create a natural visual break that softens a cut."}
    ]},

  { num:"8", color:"#C084FC", title:"Text & Captions",
    subtitle:"The overlays that stop the scroll and the captions that keep people watching.",
    lesson:
`Text is powerful because people often **read before they watch** — a hook overlay sets the tone in the first three seconds and works on mute.

## Text overlays for hooks
Replicate the inspiration's text style when the brief says so; otherwise use proven, native-looking styles. Recommended fonts, with **TikTok Sans** as the default, plus Inter ExtraBold, Georgia Regular, Proxima Nova Semibold, Helvetica Neue.

## Captions keep people watching
Everything else on screen stays still, but captions **change every second** — that keeps the viewer "in sync" and boosts retention. They must feel native to the platform.

Quick rules: **3–5 words per change**; **never** over the product, brand name, or key visuals; keep them **inside the safe zone** with consistent size and spacing; use **high-contrast colours**.

## Create captions
Generate captions with **Premiere Pro Speech-to-Text**.
- No spelling or grammar mistakes — always check against the script.
- Timing must match the spoken dialogue precisely.
- **Group words naturally** so a caption never breaks mid-thought. The "/" marks where a new caption begins:
- ✅ Correct — "I was / a doctor"
- ✅ Correct — "I was a doctor"
- ❌ Wrong — "I was a / doctor"
- ❌ Wrong — "I / was a doctor"
- Captions must stay synced with every A-roll and B-roll cut, and **never cross a scene change**.

If you use captioning software outside Premiere Pro (e.g. Submagic or CapCut): export the whole paced timeline as one video, generate captions, review the grouping, then export as a **green-screen video**. Import it back on the **topmost layer**, remove the green with a **chroma / ultra key**, and cut it at every Hook, Body, CTA, A-roll and B-roll transition so captions stay synced.

## Most common caption styles
**Style 1 — Organic TikTok**
- Font: Proxima Nova Semibold, 60–72 pt.
- Colour: usually white with a **black outline** (use a white outline if the font is dark).
- Shadow: ❌ none — shadows plus outlines (especially black) make text look blurry.
- Use: fast-paced TikTok/Shorts edits; casual but clean.

**Style 2 — TikTok Box**
- Font: Proxima Nova Semibold, 60–72 pt.
- Colour: white text on a **dark box** (black or dark grey).
- Outline: ❌ none. Shadow: ❌ none.
- Alignment: centred. Box: **all corners rounded**, including between stacked lines.
- Use: clear, attention-grabbing captions for educational, commentary, or listicle videos.

**Style 3 — Word-by-word animated (Submagic, CapCut, Captions.ai)**
- Font: clean, simple sans-serif; medium size — balanced for readability.
- Colour: **high contrast only** (no dark-on-dark or light-on-light).
- Shadow: optional, only if it improves visibility — avoid anything that blurs.
- Alignment: usually bottom centre or bottom left, depending on space.
- Animation: subtle and easy to follow, never distracting.
- Timing: each word animates in sync with speech, and subtitles end **exactly when the B-roll ends**.

> **Line structure (applies to all captions):** break lines logically, never mid-thought or mid-sentence.

> Remember: the **reference is the master**.`,
    questions:[
      {q:"Why do captions increase retention?",options:["They change constantly, keeping the viewer in sync and watching","They add background music","They replace the voiceover","They shrink the video file"],answer:0,explain:"Constantly-changing captions give the eye a reason to stay locked on the video."},
      {q:"The ideal amount of caption text per change is…",options:["3–5 words","1 word","A full sentence","10–12 words"],answer:0,explain:"3–5 words per change reads comfortably without overwhelming the frame."},
      {q:"The default font for text overlays is…",options:["TikTok Sans","Comic Sans","Times New Roman","Papyrus"],answer:0,explain:"TikTok Sans is the go-to for high-performing overlays."},
      {q:"Captions should never be placed over…",options:["The product, brand name, or key visuals","The sky or plain backgrounds","The lower third of the frame","The avatar's shoulder"],answer:0,explain:"Blocking the product or brand hurts both clarity and the ad's job."},
      {q:"Grouping caption words naturally, which split is incorrect?",options:["I / was a doctor","I was / a doctor","I was a doctor"],answer:0,explain:"Words that belong together should share a caption; splitting right after 'I' breaks readability."},
      {q:"Why cut the caption layer at every A-roll and B-roll transition?",options:["So captions stay synced with the edit and never linger across a scene change","To enlarge them","To change the font per scene","To add music"],answer:0,explain:"Captions must stay synced with every cut and never cross a scene change."}
    ]}
